WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.534
-John Oliver, it is so great to
see you.

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:03.901
-Thanks for having me.
-Welcome back to the show.

00:00:03.901 --> 00:00:05.634
Did you do anything
for the holidays?

00:00:05.634 --> 00:00:07.901
Did you get any time off before
you start going back to work?

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:10.467
-I got COVID. Does that count?
-Wow, no.

00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:11.868
-That counts.
That's something, right?

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:14.167
First time.
I thought I was indestructible.

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:15.634
I wasn't.
-Oh, gosh.

00:00:15.634 --> 00:00:17.734
-I got that. The kids got RSV.

00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:18.567
That counts as something
as well.

00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:20.801
-Oh, my gosh. This is insane.

00:00:20.801 --> 00:00:22.868
-Yeah, the Oliver family's been
ill, Jimmy.

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:25.501
-Yeah, they, really -- Yeah.
But no celebrating or --

00:00:25.501 --> 00:00:28.133
-No, we tried to celebrate
as much as we could.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.667
Then I went to Chicago to do
some stand-up, so that was nice.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:31.634
I did that over
on New Year's Eve.

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:33.000
[ Scattered cheers ]
That's --

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.534
It's not clear -- It's not clear
whether they're supporting

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:38.534
the city of Chicago
or the live arts.

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:40.534
[ Laughter ]
-The live arts.

00:00:40.534 --> 00:00:42.400
But New Year's Eve, you like
working on New Year's Eve?

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.634
-I love working
on New Year's Eve.

00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:44.934
-Really?
-Because it means you don't have

00:00:44.934 --> 00:00:46.834
to do anything else
on New Year's Eve.

00:00:46.834 --> 00:00:49.033
So it's partly 'cause I love
doing stand-up and it's fun.

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:51.200
And it's partly because it stops
me having to go to

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.067
any New Year's Eve party, where
I will not be value added.

00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:57.133
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.133 --> 00:00:59.501
-No party is going up a notch.

00:00:59.501 --> 00:01:00.901
-When you're there
on New Year's Eve.

00:01:00.901 --> 00:01:04.133
-You know, like, when, like,
LMFAO walk into a room?

00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:06.100
What a dated reference that is.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.000
[ Laughter ]
[ Rim shot ]

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.667
-That's a fantastic --
-Oh, my God.

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:11.934
-I literally -- I was thinking,
"Who, who --"

00:01:11.934 --> 00:01:13.234
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.234 --> 00:01:14.834
"Who would really elevate
a party?

00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:16.601
Those two guys from LMFAO."
You know.

00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:17.667
-Of course. Yeah.

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:18.634
-"Party rock is in the house,
tonight."

00:01:18.634 --> 00:01:21.234
-Absolutely, yes!
They are a party.

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:24.033
-"Everybody just have
a good time."

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:25.300
[ Rhythmic clapping ]

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:26.667
-Yeah, with the glasses.
-Yeah.

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:28.234
Picture that.
-Yeah.

00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:30.300
-Picture the opposite of that,
and you're looking at it.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.567
-That's you. Alright, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:33.501
So, are you on stage
at midnight?

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:35.834
-No, I would not do that.

00:01:35.834 --> 00:01:38.033
I did it once, actually,
the last time I was in Chicago.

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:40.667
The reason I stopped being
on stage at midnight

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.300
was I had to do the countdown.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.868
And there was a guy there
and he was in the front row.

00:01:44.868 --> 00:01:47.701
And I was about to do the 10 of
the 10 seconds.

00:01:47.701 --> 00:01:52.601
And he put this bottle down, and
he said, "I've got some Malort."

00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:55.200
I didn't know what Malort was.
It's like --

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.267
[ Audience groans ]
Oh, it's not good.

00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:58.667
[ Laughter ]
-Malort?

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:01.834
-It's like a fizzy wine.
-No.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:03.901
-No? What is it?
-It's terrible.

00:02:03.901 --> 00:02:05.801
-It's terrible, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:07.634
-Thank you.
That's a nice way of putting it.

00:02:07.634 --> 00:02:09.100
"It's not a fizzy wine.
It's terrible."

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.033
-You're not even willing to
describe it more than that.

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:12.167
[ Laughter ]
Just "it's terrible."

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:13.767
-It's just terrible.
-It's a bad liquid.

00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:15.234
You are right.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:17.934
I think it's made --
Yeah, he put it down.

00:02:17.934 --> 00:02:20.934
And then he said, "Drink this,"
like a bully.

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:23.267
And then a guy at the other side
of the theater, who,

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:24.901
I can't stress this enough,
had not come with him,

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:27.767
ran down the side, and he had,
like, one of those, like,

00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:30.367
bullet belts with shot glasses
in it.

00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:33.133
So he'd worn that to a show.

00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:36.133
And to my show,
not to LMFAO's show...

00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:37.801
[ Laughter ]
...where it might be welcome.

00:02:37.801 --> 00:02:39.868
-He wore a vest of shot glasses?
-Yeah.

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:43.901
And so he said -- So, he put his
shot glasses on the front of

00:02:43.901 --> 00:02:47.033
the stage, thinking I'm like
Jesus at sermon of the mount.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:49.167
"I will take this
one bottle of Malort

00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:50.667
and get this room
[bleep]-faced."

00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:52.701
-Yeah, exactly, yeah.
-And yeah.

00:02:52.701 --> 00:02:55.000
So, I took one sip.
It was so unpleasant,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.200
"terrible," to quote
your audience member,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.834
that I failed to count down
from 10 to 1 accurately.

00:03:01.834 --> 00:03:05.534
I missed New Year's Eve
and ruined everybody's night.

00:03:05.534 --> 00:03:07.634
[ Laughter ]
-Don't do that ever, ever again.

00:03:07.634 --> 00:03:09.434
[ Applause ]
Please, don't do that.

00:03:09.434 --> 00:03:11.968
-Yeah. So, no, I was done. Yeah.

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:14.567
-A few months ago,
you hosted this event

00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:16.367
for the Clooney Foundation.

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:18.601
-I did. Yeah, the Albie Awards.
It was fantastic.

00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:20.767
They honored Maria Ressa,
Albie Sachs.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.801
It was an incredible night.
-When George Clooney calls

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:25.300
and asks to do this, do you go,
"Oh, yeah, whatever you need"?

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.701
-Yeah. I don't know if I'm
really at the point in my life

00:03:27.701 --> 00:03:30.934
where you can take an unexpected
call from George Clooney

00:03:30.934 --> 00:03:32.601
and go, "Yeah, no."

00:03:32.601 --> 00:03:33.834
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.834 --> 00:03:34.901
-Yeah, so it was -- but it was

00:03:34.901 --> 00:03:37.334
an amazing, amazing organization
they are.

00:03:37.334 --> 00:03:39.400
And they were celebrating
some incredible people.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.968
And it was -- yeah, it was
a magical, spectacular night.

00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:44.567
-You had a great table.
-I did.

00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:45.767
-Yeah.
-What do you got?

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:47.434
-Well, I have a picture of --
-Oh, yeah!

00:03:47.434 --> 00:03:49.767
-You and Meryl Streep
having a blast.

00:03:49.767 --> 00:03:53.567
-Yes. Now, I found out about
this photo 'cause one of

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:56.400
our writers dropped that photo
the next day

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.133
in our Slack channel with
the message, in all caps,

00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:01.000
"What the [bleep]
was happening here?"

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.167
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:05.067
That felt like a fair question.

00:04:05.067 --> 00:04:07.300
And honestly, I don't have
the answer to it.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.334
I don't know what Meryl Streep
had said.

00:04:09.334 --> 00:04:11.400
I will say, that is typically
not the dynamic

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.300
a comedian is looking for.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.667
It's supposed to be
the other way round.

00:04:15.667 --> 00:04:19.200
I'm supposed to be
making Streep laugh.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.334
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:22.534
-Meryl Streep is
cracking you up.

00:04:22.534 --> 00:04:25.133
-I don't know what she said,
but she was destroying.

00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:26.601
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:29.000
-That's really funny.
-Yeah. She's clearly very funny.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.200
That's a photographic evidence
artifact.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.634
-I wanted to ask you about
another photo.

00:04:32.634 --> 00:04:35.334
And I'm gonna show it, not
because it's a cute baby photo.

00:04:35.334 --> 00:04:37.234
-What is it?
-It is a cute baby, but --

00:04:37.234 --> 00:04:39.000
-Oh.
-It's a cute baby, but it's you,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.434
but you're wearing a sweater
that has your full name on it.

00:04:41.434 --> 00:04:43.267
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. I don't know.

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:45.100
-I've never seen that
in my life.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.033
-I don't know how that got
on to the Internet.

00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:49.434
But, yeah, that is me,
as you can tell.

00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:51.167
By the sweater, not the face.

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:53.234
And, yeah, I think what is
amazing,

00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:56.300
is that I don't remember,
either, who knitted that,

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.200
but I think it is a bold choice
to go --

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.801
-Full name.
-Full name is a bold choice.

00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:02.901
First name, cute.
-It's so cute.

00:05:02.901 --> 00:05:04.734
-Full name feels like you're
about to put

00:05:04.734 --> 00:05:06.200
the phone number underneath it.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.167
That feels like, "If lost,
please deliver to this address."

00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:12.534
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:05:12.534 --> 00:05:14.634
-Did this kid --
-More people should do that.

00:05:14.634 --> 00:05:17.400
-Did this kid think that he was
going to host a show

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.901
and win awards and be one of
the funniest people on Earth?

00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:22.200
-I think that kid knew
one thing,

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.834
and that's that his name was
John Oliver.

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:26.534
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:30.868
No, no. That kid was not aware
of that fact, at all.

00:05:30.868 --> 00:05:34.067
-But were you into comedy
originally or in acting?

00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:35.400
-Oh, comedy.
-Yeah.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.968
-I mean, I acted in school
plays, but, you know,

00:05:38.968 --> 00:05:40.801
I don't think we need to bring
up how good I was

00:05:40.801 --> 00:05:43.100
as Fagin in "Oliver."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.701
-Hold your applause.

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:45.901
-Oh, they held it.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:48.167
Even this audience,
which is supportive,

00:05:48.167 --> 00:05:50.300
were going, "That sounds like
any child."

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:54.467
Yeah, so I liked it,
but no, I'm not an actor.

00:05:54.467 --> 00:05:58.300
In fact, I did -- I did a show
called "Community" for a while.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:02.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.667
I think I realized just how
little of an actor I was

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:07.033
when I was doing a scene with
Gillian Jacobs in a car

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:09.000
and she was crying
in this scene,

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.434
and I remember just watching her
thinking,

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:13.100
"Oh, look, you're crying now.
That's a real --"

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.868
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.868 --> 00:06:17.467
"It's got a real tear.
You look really sad.

00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:19.701
And yet we were just talking
and you weren't sad,

00:06:19.701 --> 00:06:22.033
but now you're being sad,
and the tear rolled down."

00:06:22.033 --> 00:06:23.234
And I was watching the tear go
down her face.

00:06:23.234 --> 00:06:24.501
-And you're in the scene
with her?

00:06:24.501 --> 00:06:27.501
-And it hit me, "Oh, I'm giving
this individual

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:30.701
nothing to work with,
absolutely nothing."

00:06:30.701 --> 00:06:31.968
-Disaster.
-Yeah.

00:06:31.968 --> 00:06:34.767
-"You're a real actor."
-Yeah, she did not need

00:06:34.767 --> 00:06:37.534
anyone -- You know when you see
like behind the scenes

00:06:37.534 --> 00:06:40.234
of movies and there's, like,
an actor acting opposite

00:06:40.234 --> 00:06:41.434
a tennis ball?
-Yes.

00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:43.400
-I was that tennis ball.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.334
-That's very nice of you.
-We did once actually

00:06:45.334 --> 00:06:48.033
on the show, we got
these wax presidents,

00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:51.601
and Laura Linney was acting
opposite a wax

00:06:51.601 --> 00:06:54.667
Warren G. Harding,
and she was acting so well

00:06:54.667 --> 00:06:56.801
you realize, "Oh, you've just
been carrying

00:06:56.801 --> 00:06:58.367
actors your entire career."

00:06:58.367 --> 00:06:59.667
[ Laughter ]
-She's that good.

00:06:59.667 --> 00:07:01.634
-She is that good, yeah.
-She's that good.

00:07:01.634 --> 00:07:03.400
-Let's talk about
"Last Week Tonight."

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.601
It's going into season 10.
-Sure. Yes.

00:07:05.601 --> 00:07:08.067
[ Cheers and applause ]
Double figures. Double figures.

00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:10.968
Big 10. Big 10, yeah.
-Wow, double -- Yeah.

00:07:10.968 --> 00:07:13.968
Does it feel like 10 seasons?

00:07:13.968 --> 00:07:15.701
-Not really, no,
'cause we haven't --

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.100
You know, we haven't
really stopped.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.234
So it kind of feels
like one long splurge of work.

00:07:21.234 --> 00:07:23.767
-Yeah.
-Yeah, the only time it really

00:07:23.767 --> 00:07:26.133
hit me just how long it's been,
is that we were

00:07:26.133 --> 00:07:30.767
asking the writers for ideas
for our new poster this year.

00:07:30.767 --> 00:07:33.934
And one of our writers, Seena,
he said, "I got one for you."

00:07:33.934 --> 00:07:36.868
And he just said, "Let's just
take a photo of you now

00:07:36.868 --> 00:07:39.033
and a photo from
our original poster.

00:07:39.033 --> 00:07:40.534
And then we'll --" Yeah.

00:07:40.534 --> 00:07:45.567
So, this is what our poster
is for the coming season.

00:07:45.567 --> 00:07:48.000
"It's only been nine years."
So, that's --

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.701
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:07:49.701 --> 00:07:55.067
That's me nine years ago.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:55.067 --> 00:07:58.567
That, Jimmy.
-Nine years. That's it.

00:07:58.567 --> 00:08:01.267
-I don't think you need to be
an official doctor

00:08:01.267 --> 00:08:03.067
to know that this is not ideal.

00:08:03.067 --> 00:08:05.267
[ Laughter ]

00:08:05.267 --> 00:08:07.868
That is not aging
at its ideal form.

00:08:07.868 --> 00:08:10.133
When you're zero to 9,
you're allowed to change.

00:08:10.133 --> 00:08:11.868
Baby, child, fine.

00:08:11.868 --> 00:08:13.300
9 to 18, you're allowed to
change.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:15.901
I don't think nine years can do
that to a human being

00:08:15.901 --> 00:08:18.067
unless you are President of
the United States.

00:08:18.067 --> 00:08:21.734
-No, no.
-This guy had his whole life

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.601
ahead of him. Look at him.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.601 --> 00:08:26.634
He's thinking, "Obama's
the president right now.

00:08:26.634 --> 00:08:28.100
Sure, he's a little
technocratic,

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.467
disappointing in some ways,

00:08:29.467 --> 00:08:31.367
but it seems like America's
on the right track.

00:08:31.367 --> 00:08:34.000
There hasn't been a pandemic
since 1918.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.501
What's the worst
that could happen?"

00:08:35.501 --> 00:08:37.934
This guy [bleep] around
and found out.

00:08:37.934 --> 00:08:42.267
[ Laughter ]
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:42.267 --> 00:08:45.300
-Unbelievable. Come on, man.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.033
John Oliver, everybody.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:47.033 --> 00:08:49.534
John and I are playing
a new game after the break.

00:08:49.534 --> 00:08:50.968
Stick around. Come on back.
-Yes.

